1. Inspect the entire existing codebase before making changes.
2. Understand the current architecture, data models, business rules, storage mechanisms, authentication, authorization, and component relationships.
3. Identify existing defects before modifying code.
4. Fix the underlying causes, not just the visible symptoms.
5. Preserve existing working functionality, user data, accounts, and application identity.
6. Do not rebuild the application from scratch.
7. Do not replace existing business logic with simplified mock implementations.
8. Do not introduce dummy transactions, fake products, sample orders, or fabricated analytics into real user accounts.
