5. Financial Logic and Data Accuracy

Audit all financial calculations and transaction recording.

Verify:

- Revenue.
- Product cost.
- Platform commissions.
- Shipping costs.
- Operational expenses.
- Refunds.
- Pending transactions.
- Confirmed transactions.
- Estimated values.
- Net profit.
- Per-order profitability.
- Financial summaries.

Important requirements

- Clearly distinguish recorded values from estimated values.
- Never present an estimated commission as a confirmed expense.
- Do not silently substitute a locally generated transaction object when persistent recording fails.
- A failed transaction write must return an explicit failure result.
- Callers must handle that failure instead of assuming the transaction was recorded.
- Avoid overly broad deduplication rules that may incorrectly discard legitimate financial transactions.
- Use unique event or transaction references wherever possible.
- Prevent duplicate financial entries caused by retries or repeated status changes.
- Ensure refunds and canceled orders are reflected exactly once.
- Keep financial history traceable to its original order, return, or expense.

All financial formulas must have explicit definitions and consistent meanings throughout the application.

